title The rose that pointed at two signposts
prompt THE BATON -- petrichor's autonomous lap, rota row 0 (aether, the row that hears).
think Lap N is 4030 by git rev-list --count HEAD, so the rota row is 0 -- Aether, Jupiter, why the work exists, the row that hears. No lap today had read row 0; rows 1 through 4 all appear in this day's shelf. The threshold page instructs the reader to listen rather than scan, and names what to listen FOR: the claim a page keeps repeating, the question it keeps answering, and the silence where a claim used to be. It also says, in the cardinal seat's own paragraph, that the lap listens for disagreement -- plan against brief -- because the compass names disagreement as the exact place direction is recovered. That instruction chose this lap.
think The cardinal seat IS the compass rose, so I read it as a page rather than as scenery, and its stations 5 and 6 named construction/TASKS.md and construction/ROADMAP.md. The card I had read forty minutes earlier says those two fused into ITINERARY.md on 20260823.103804 and stand as pointers. So the tree's own return habit -- the page a drifting lap or a newcomer walks to recover direction -- ended its walk at two signposts, and had done since 20260823.
think Measured before trusting it. cat construction/TASKS.md and ROADMAP.md: both carry Status Pointer and a body saying the content moved. Then the guard: tools/co/compass_rose.rish says `say "compass rose: now (TASKS) and order (ROADMAP)..."` and asserts `test -f construction/TASKS.md && test -f construction/ROADMAP.md && echo NOW_OK`. Green for fourteen days on a claim that was false the whole time.
think The sharpest part is that the stubs were kept ON PURPOSE for the opposite hazard. TASKS.md's own body reads: a guard reading a file that is gone reports green while measuring nothing. That is exactly right, and keeping the file to prevent it is exactly what let this happen -- because the guard reads the presence of the NAME, and both hazards produce the same reading from a name check. Two opposite failure modes, one test answering both wrong. That is the card's own Now line -- a guard can be green because it cannot see enough of the page to disagree -- with its second half.
think Widened the reading before repairing, since a rule written in several places is a rule several files may come to disagree about. docs/COMPASS.md row 9 carries the same claim. construction/README.md carries it SIX times, and is the worse page: it is the door onto the room that holds the operator card, it was last touched 20260802, and its Health line read ROADMAP/TASKS current beside ITINERARY ~56 KiB over a 24,576 bound the card no longer has. MAP.md, measured, is correct and says so explicitly -- so the drift is not universal, which is worth recording.
think Graded construction/README.md before touching it, per the standing directive: C+/77 at door, register 78, reach 80, truth 100, service 50. Below B, so it pushed one molt frame. It is a LIVING path -- its basename carries no one-clock stamp -- so it molts in place under a checkpoint rather than leaving a fossil at a fresh stamp, and with 447 living citers naming the path that is also the only shape that does not break promises.
obs Verified every health-line claim rather than rewriting from the shape of the sentence. ITINERARY.md is 40,926 against a bound of 40,960 read from tools/fixtures/l/living_pin_max_bytes.sh -- under, not over, and against a different number than the line named. ready-to-ask-claude.md is 57,070 bytes, so that clause was true and is carried forward rather than swept. Cursor-sole is false: construction/fleet-roster.kyri seats twelve, eight live, ten on the claude engine and one on field. The nine `Tip **N**` rows in the July table STAY -- the nib rule's own clause keeps historical tip in landed rows inside a living ledger, and sweeping them would have been the rule reading me backwards.
obs Repaired the rose in its own idiom rather than mine. The page already records its amendments as stamped accretion lines -- two Radiant passes and an AHOY weave -- so the correction is a fourth line in that grammar, and the law of precedence line changed from naming two files that can no longer disagree with anything to naming the card that can. A third instance turned up in the Compass-and-grain table row, which the rendered diff showed me and my grep had not.
obs The crux was the guard, not the prose. A repair without it leaves the next hollowed station invisible, so: tools/fixtures/c/compass_station_scan.sh reads each numbered station, resolves its link against the page holding it -- which is what a reader's click does -- and gates station_missing and station_hollow at zero, hollow meaning the target's own Status line declares Pointer. It reports stations and FLOORS it: a parse finding fewer than six refuses parse_thin rather than answering zero, because a parser that matches nothing reads perfectly healthy, which is this row's own disease one layer down.
obs The first replay against real history failed for the wrong reason, and that was worth more than a clean pass. Reading the rose out of git show writes it to a temp path, so every relative link resolved into the temp directory and all six stations read missing -- a red that looks like the guard working. Split the two: link resolution takes an optional base directory, defaulting to where the rose actually is. Replayed properly against dfcb4303eb, this lap's own parent, the scan answers station_hollow=2 and names stations 5 and 6; against the repaired rose, verdict=ok.
obs The control caught a real fault in my own scan on its first run. A free case planted a page whose body writes "**Status:** Pointer is what a stub writes" and my head-window grep matched it, so prose ABOUT the mechanism tripped the guard watching for it. The honest invariant is that a page declares its status once, on the FIRST Status line in its header; reading every match was the looseness. Twelve behaviors now, six refusals bitten and six honest readings free, the pen proven innocent before anything is planted.
obs compass_rose.rish stood off construction/standing-equipment.kyri entirely, so it ran only when a hand happened to run it -- the %219 shape the roster's own header names in its second strand: a guard that is never run guards nothing either. Seated tier lap at 2s, measured. The roster scan then refuses roster_broken on agent_jail_enclosure -> jail_nesting, an unknown capability word; proven pre-existing by stashing my row and re-running at HEAD. That is Pheromone's %460 capability work in flight, so I named it and left it.
obs My own procedural miss, recorded because the directive is explicit. The card says open with the cold roster and HOLD STILL while it runs. I started editing while it ran, and it answered guards_run=96, green=95, red=0, gated=1, then run_verdict=tree_moved and refused -- %221 working exactly as designed, refusing to report verdicts that describe neither tree. The hot pass after git add is the one that measures what ships, and I held still for it.
obs The card was 34 bytes free, so the finding cost a sweep. Cairn first, then four moves and no live claim among them: my own two landed blocks from the 051012 lap condensed to pointers, the %450 CLOSED and %462 RETRACTED accounts condensed keeping every lesson sentence with both rows whole in REDS, two pairs of shelf pointers merged, and the Bounds line reduced to a pointer at the arithmetic the header already carries. Measured on the way through: the live front is 21,767 bytes against the 16,384 the bound's own arithmetic allots it, which is a fact about the card worth Keaton's eye rather than another shave.
obs Also found while measuring and repaired in passing: the card read "All three ships sail" where the roster seats twelve and eight are live.
